  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the global mercury budget between land and ocean, emphasizing how much mercury is emitted to the atmosphere, redeposited to land, and exchanged with the sea. On land, total terrestrial emissions are shown as about 4,120 Mg per year, with the largest contribution from anthropogenic sources (about 2,220), followed by geogenic and legacy soils/vegetation (about 1,300), and biomass burning (about 600). These upward arrows indicate mercury release from the land surface and human activities back to the atmosphere.\n\nTerrestrial deposition is smaller, around 2,800 Mg per year overall, and is split into dry deposition and wet deposition. Dry deposition appears to dominate, accounting for roughly 62–74% of the total, while wet deposition contributes about 26–38%. Most dry deposition goes to vegetation rather than non-vegetated surfaces, and the vegetation pathway is mainly elemental mercury, Hg(0), with a smaller fraction as Hg(II).\n\nThe lower part of the graphic highlights soil and ocean mercury pools as major reservoirs. Over the ocean, deposition exceeds evasion: about 3,600 Mg per year is deposited to the ocean while about 2,300 escapes back to the atmosphere, yielding a net ocean deposition of roughly 1,300 Mg per year.",
